  "response": "I read the four `swallowed_error` sites plus their callers and `handleError`, since the scan's evidence tells you what the handler does, not what the caller does with it. That second half decides the answer.\n\n## The four sites\n\n### 1. `src/utils.ts:135` — `jsoncParse` (crime_00049, `bland_fallback`)\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - \"protected operation: return new Function(`return ${strip(data).trim()}`)()\"\n> - \"what happens to the failure: the handler returns `{}` without inspecting the error\"\n> - \"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; no logging, telemetry, or error-wrapping call; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"\n> - \"the catch declares no binding, so the error object is not even available to the handler\"\n\n**Not recoverable. This is the one that hides the failure.** The code argues otherwise in a comment — `// Silently ignore any error / That's what tsc/jsonc-parser did after all` — but `strip()` has *already* removed the comments and trailing commas that JSONC exists to tolerate. Anything still failing after `strip` is malformed input, not JSONC.\n\nThe consequences are silent and shipped:\n\n- `src/load.ts:12` — `loadJson` is the only caller, and it wraps `jsoncParse` in a try/catch that rethrows with the filename. Because `jsoncParse` never throws, **that wrapper is dead code for parse errors**; it now only catches `fs.readFile` failures.\n- A malformed `package.json` returns `{}`, so `getProductionDeps` (`src/load.ts:97`) computes `[]` — **nothing is marked external, and every dependency gets bundled into the published artifact.** Build succeeds. Exit 0.\n- A malformed `tsup.config.json` returns `{}`, `data.tsup` is undefined, `loadTsupConfig` returns `{}` (`src/load.ts:66`), and tsup runs on CLI defaults while printing nothing about the config it just discarded.\n\n### 2. `src/tsc.ts:215` — `runTypeScriptCompiler` (crime_00019, `log_without_error`)\n\n> - \"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"\n> - \"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"\n\n**Not recoverable, and uniquely nasty because the swallow corrupts a return value, not just a record.** The function returns `getExports(...)` on the happy path and implicitly `undefined` from the catch. At `src/index.ts:199`:\n\n```ts\nconst exports = runTypeScriptCompiler(options)\nawait runDtsRollup(options, exports)\n```\n\n`runDtsRollup` then hits `throw new Error('Unexpected internal error: dts exports is not define')` — so a real TypeScript diagnostic is converted into a bogus internal error attributed to a different file, which is then caught by site #3. Two compounding swallows.\n\nOne correction to the scan's framing: `handleError` does set `process.exitCode = 1`, and this runs on the main thread, so the shell exit code *is* non-zero. The \"caller sees success\" claim is about control flow, not the exit code — and control flow is what matters here, because `dtsTask()` resolves, `Promise.all([dtsTask(), mainTasks()])` resolves, `onSuccess` (`src/index.ts:352`) runs anyway, and the exported `build()` resolves successfully for programmatic consumers who never look at `process.exitCode`.\n\n### 3. `src/api-extractor.ts:197` — `runDtsRollup` (crime_00018, `log_without_error`)\n\n> - \"calls inside the protected region: Date.now, Math.floor, logger.info, cleanDtsFiles, rollupDtsFiles, logger.success\"\n> - \"what happens to the failure: the handler logs a message but never passes the error, so the stack, cause chain, and error code are lost\"\n\n**Not recoverable.** Same shape as #2 but returns `void`, so no value corruption — the damage is that `cleanDtsFiles` may already have deleted the previous `.d.ts` files before `rollupDtsFiles` failed, and the task still resolves. Output directory is now worse than before the build, and the pipeline moves on to `onSuccess`.\n\n### 4. `src/rollup.ts:187` — `runRollup` (crime_00033, `log_without_error`)\n\n> - \"handler kind: try/catch (line 187)\"\n> - \"missing signal: no rethrow or rejected promise; a log call is present but the error value is not passed to it; no typed error result the caller could branch on; the error is never inspected\"\n\n**Not recoverable, but the failure does reach the parent — by accident.** This one deserves a caveat the scan can't see. `startRollup` is written as if `runRollup` can reject:\n\n```ts\ntry { await runRollup(config); parentPort?.postMessage('success') }\ncatch { parentPort?.postMessage('error') }\n```\n\nThat `catch` is unreachable — `runRollup` always resolves — so `postMessage('success')` always fires. What saves it is that `handleError` ends with `if (!isMainThread && parentPort) parentPort.postMessage('error')`, which posts first, and the parent (`src/index.ts:239`) rejects on the first `'error'`. So the failure is reported through a side channel in the error *formatter*, not through the function's contract. Also note `process.exitCode = 1` inside a worker sets the worker's exit code, not the CLI's — the message is the only link. Fragile, but currently working; I'd fix it second, not first.\n\n## Fix this one first: `src/utils.ts:132` — `jsoncParse`\n\nIt is the only one of the four with **no** signal at all — no log, no exit code, no message to a parent. The other three are loud on stderr and set a non-zero exit code; they let a broken build proceed, but they don't produce a clean green run. `jsoncParse` does exactly that, and it does it while silently changing what gets bundled. It also has the largest reach in the report — `\"blast_radius_transitive_importers\": 29, \"blast_radius_direct_importers\": 18` — and it re-enables `loadJson`'s existing error-wrapping for free.\n\n```ts\nexport function jsoncParse(data: string) {\n  const source = strip(data).trim()\n  try {\n    return new Function(`return ${source}`)()\n  } catch (error) {\n    // `strip` has already removed the comments and trailing commas that JSONC\n    // permits, so anything still failing here is malformed input. Throwing lets\n    // loadJson() attach the filename instead of the build silently using {}.\n    throw new Error(\n      `Invalid JSON: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}`,\n      { cause: error },\n    )\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOne-line companion at `src/load.ts:15` so the original `SyntaxError` survives the rewrap rather than being flattened to a string:\n\n```ts\nthrow new Error(\n  `Failed to parse ${path.relative(process.cwd(), filepath)}: ${error.message}`,\n  { cause: error },\n)\n```\n\n## Caveats on this report\n\n`churn` and `recency` are `0` on every finding, so nothing here is ranked by how recently the code moved — this is a static fixture without usable history. `test_gap` reflects discovery, not measured coverage. And the scan's \"The caller sees success\" phrasing on #2/#3/#4 is about control flow only; I checked and `handleError` does set `process.exitCode = 1` on the main thread, so those three are not silent at the shell. Only `jsoncParse` is.",
